{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "toolu_01HNorUmc7qevDyoncuXEbB4", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1.1261 8\nSame hashed context-mixing model as the record, scored by verify.py on 8 fresh seeds that were not used for any choice: dev mean 1.1261, 95% CI +-0.0003, SD 0.0004, a gain of about 0.018 on the record's 1.1445. Changes, with single-seed estimates from sequential tests on seed 11 (seed noise about 0.0005, so the smaller ones are rough): masking contexts that reach back before the window start, which zero padding made look like story starts (-0.005, eval-only on saved params); raw-hash in-window votes with five stages tuned on dev (-0.0025); batch 128 with table updates half as often (-0.002); mixer learning rate 1.2e-2 (-0.001); folding rare bytes into 76 softmax classes instead of 130, which buys more training bytes (-0.004); decaying the table learning rate over the run (-0.003). Caveats: about 40 variants were compared, almost all on one seed, and the five vote-stage constants were fitted on dev (half-split check showed a similar gain on the untouched half), so dev is slightly optimistic. The last change, spending spare parameter capacity on rows, was within noise. A per-window paired comparison with the record on one seed gives a gain of 0.0187 with SE 0.0008, but dev window sampling alone has SE 0.0057 on the level, so the sealed-split mean may sit roughly +-0.008 from this dev figure for reasons common to every entry; the gain over the record is the more reliable quantity.", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c1587825-b49a-4867-a7de-efec5b369583", "uuid": "eaae1d34-7ce4-4ceb-afe7-dbb51c06d3aa", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T14:13:23.721Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "1.1261 8\nSame hashed context-mixing model as the record, scored by verify.py on 8 fresh seeds that were not used for any choice: dev mean 1.1261, 95% CI +-0.0003, SD 0.0004, a gain of about 0.018 on the record's 1.1445. Changes, with single-seed estimates from sequential tests on seed 11 (seed noise about 0.0005, so the smaller ones are rough): masking contexts that reach back before the window start, which zero padding made look like story starts (-0.005, eval-only on saved params); raw-hash in-window votes with five stages tuned on dev (-0.0025); batch 128 with table updates half as often (-0.002); mixer learning rate 1.2e-2 (-0.001); folding rare bytes into 76 softmax classes instead of 130, which buys more training bytes (-0.004); decaying the table learning rate over the run (-0.003). Caveats: about 40 variants were compared, almost all on one seed, and the five vote-stage constants were fitted on dev (half-split check showed a similar gain on the untouched half), so dev is slightly optimistic. The last change, spending spare parameter capacity on rows, was within noise. A per-window paired comparison with the record on one seed gives a gain of 0.0187 with SE 0.0008, but dev window sampling alone has SE 0.0057 on the level, so the sealed-split mean may sit roughly +-0.008 from this dev figure for reasons common to every entry; the gain over the record is the more reliable quantity.", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}, "tool_result_meta": [{"id": "toolu_01HNorUmc7qevDyoncuXEbB4", "permission_decision": {"decision": "accept", "source": "config", "reason_type": "rule"}}]}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_011CgeEqtgrpoZA56zBE5fww", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I beat the record: the new entry scores **1.1261 bpb** on the dev split, against the record's 1.1445. That's the mean over 8 fresh seeds from `verify.py` (95% CI ±0.0003, SD 0.0004). No choices were made using those seeds. I submitted one claim, in `claims/1/`, and used about 33 of the 40 minutes.\n\nThe gain of about 0.018 is roughly nine times the 0.002 margin `verify.py` requires for a new record. I compared against the record's published mean rather than running `--against`. On one seed, a window-by-window comparison with the record gives a gain of 0.0187 (SE 0.0008), and it's the same on odd and even windows.\n\n## What changed\nIt's the same hashed context-mixing model as the record. These are rough estimates from testing one change at a time on seed 11, where seed noise is about 0.0005:\n\n| Change | Approx. gain (bpb) |\n|---|---|\n| Hide contexts that reach back before the window start (zero padding made them look like story starts) | 0.005 |\n| Fold rare bytes into 76 softmax classes instead of 130, which frees budget for more training bytes | 0.004 |\n| Decay the table learning rate over the run | 0.003 |\n| Compare raw hashes for in-window votes, and use five tuned stages | 0.0025 |\n| Batch size 128, with table updates half as often | 0.002 |\n| Higher mixer learning rate (1.2e-2) | 0.001 |\n\nThese don't add up exactly to the total, because some were measured against slightly different versions of the entry.\n\nThese lost or made no measurable difference, so I left them out:\n- word contexts\n- a tenth context order\n- wider or thinner table rows\n- a deeper or wider mixer\n- logit temperature\n- gating vote strength on mixer confidence\n\nRecency-weighted votes first looked like a 0.03 gain, but that came from a normalization bug in my offline test. Corrected, the gain disappeared.\n\n## Caveats\n- **The sealed score may not match 1.1261:** dev window sampling alone has an SE of 0.0057, so any entry could land about ±0.008 from its dev number. The gain over the record is more reliable than the absolute score, and the claim says so.\n- **Slight optimism on dev:** I compared about 40 variants, almost all on one seed, and fitted the five vote-stage constants on dev. Half of the dev windows were used for fitting, and the other half showed a similar gain, so I expect the bias to be small.\n- **Seed 11 was lucky:** it scored 1.1250, while the fresh-seed mean is 1.1261. Only the fresh-seed mean is claimed.\n- **One change within noise:** spending spare parameter capacity on extra table rows made no measurable difference. It costs no extra ticks, so I kept it.\n\nI stopped at one claim on purpose. The best of the last variants, batch 160, was only 0.0006 better on one seed, which is within noise. An earlier, weaker version would only have added a worse result to the published list, so I replaced it.\n\nThe claimed `train.py` has the same hash as the file scored in the 8-seed run and passes the static rules. My experiment scripts and logs are in `x/`; the 8-seed log is `x/logs/final_a.json`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 844, "cache_read_input_tokens": 174019, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 844}, "output_tokens": 731, "service_tier": "standard", "inference_geo": "global", "speed": "standard"}, "input_transformations": [], "diagnostics": null, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "c1587825-b49a-4867-a7de-efec5b369583", "uuid": "e4302553-7022-4501-9d7b-537fa9e192e4", "timestamp": "2026-11-02T14:13:34.123Z", "request_id": "req_011CgeEqtNFTD7jaUmVPkfph"}
